You saved a household budget in a shared directory, but now
you want a private copy. While checking you discover a
different problem: a document you can read but cannot save.
Should you change the file, the directory, both, or neither?
Unix records different kinds of access on each object. Start
by learning to read that evidence before changing anything.

\begin{objectives}
\item Read owner, group, and other permissions in a long listing.
\item Explain why opening a file and removing its name are different.
\item Change the mode of a disposable file and predict new-file defaults.
\end{objectives}

\section{Make a room for the practice}
\label{sec:33-setup}
Use an ordinary, unprivileged account. If you are already an
administrator working in an elevated shell, open a normal
terminal instead: permission checks can behave differently with
elevated privileges. Use \cmd|sh| syntax throughout. These
commands create new names only after a fresh directory has been
made and entered. Run the first line separately; continue only if
it prints the practice directory and no error. The command
substitution lets the shell save the new pathname. \cmd|-d|
asks the widely available but non-POSIX \cmd|mktemp| utility for
a directory; the relative name avoids differences in default
temporary locations. The last six characters are replaced.

\begin{terminal}
room=$(mktemp -d ./utp-mode.XXXXXX) && cd "$room" && pwd
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminal}
mkdir -m 700 ./folder
printf 'fictional lunch budget\n' > ./folder/budget.txt
ls -ld ./folder
ls -l ./folder/budget.txt
\end{terminal}

The \cmd|mkdir -m 700| form requests a directory with all
three owner bits and no group or other bits. We'll decode
those digits below. \cmd|ls -l| asks for the long form, and \cmd|-d| shows the
named directory itself rather than listing its contents. Look
at the first field on each line. The first character identifies
the file type: \texttt{d} for a directory and \texttt{-} for
an ordinary file. After it come three sets of three positions:
owner, group, and other. The owner and group columns farther
along the same row tell you to whom the first two sets refer.
If the system appends a marker after the permission characters,
check \ManPage{ls}{1}; it may indicate additional information
such as an access control list or extended attributes, depending
on the system.

\index{permissions}
\index{owner}
\index{group}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-33/mode.tex}
\caption{Read a mode from left to right, keeping the type
separate from the three permission sets.}
\label{fig:33-mode}
\VisualDescription{Four labeled boxes show \texttt{-}, \texttt{rw-},
\texttt{r--}, and \texttt{---}: type, owner, group, and other.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:33-mode} describes a file whose owner can read
and write, whose group can read, and whose remaining users get
none of these mode permissions. A hyphen in one of the nine
slots means that permission bit is not set. Use the next
command to compare your account with the first listing.

\begin{terminal}
id
\end{terminal}

Typical \cmd|id| output includes \texttt{uid}, your numeric user
identifier, \texttt{gid}, your primary group identifier, and a
list of groups. Names may accompany numbers. These groups can
help explain why two accounts see different results. Don't
publish your account and group listing when asking for help;
``owner'', ``group member'', and ``other'' can usually describe
the case without naming anyone. In the basic mode check, if
you own the object the owner class applies; you don't give
yourself a different class by also being in its group.

\begin{quicktable}{M{.67in}YY}
\toprule
Bit & On an ordinary file & On a directory\\
\midrule
r & Read its contents. & Read the list of entry names.\\
w & Modify its contents. & Modify entries, normally also requiring
search permission.\\
x & Request execution as a program, if otherwise runnable. &
Search or traverse it when resolving a path.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{What a directory lets you do}
\label{sec:33-directory}
\index{directory!search permission}
Say you know there is a file named \texttt{budget.txt} inside
\texttt{folder}. Directory read permission is about listing
names. Directory search permission is about going through the
directory to reach a named entry. To read data at
\texttt{folder/budget.txt}, you need to traverse the directories
on the path and also pass the file's own read check. The outer
directory matters, too. A file whose mode allows everyone to
read is not thereby reachable by everyone if the directory
containing it is private.
Follow Figure~\ref{fig:33-path} in order: reaching the outer
directory tells you nothing yet about the inner one.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-33/path.tex}
\caption{Access to data can fail before the file is reached.}
\label{fig:33-path}
\VisualDescription{A reader moves through the practice directory
and folder, then reaches the file. Both directory arrows require
search; reading the contents requires file read permission.}
\end{figure}

Removing a file's name changes a directory: it is not a write
into the contents of the file. A read-only file may still be
removable if the containing directory permits a change. The
utility performing the removal can prompt in some cases; that
prompt isn't a substitute for access rules or a backup.
Directories can also have a sticky bit. In a shared writable
directory it restricts who can remove or rename entries even
when several people can write there; commonly the entry owner,
directory owner, and privileged users can do so. None of this
makes a shared directory a reliable place for the only copy of
a document. Editors may save by replacing a directory entry
rather than by updating bytes in place. That helps explain how
one application can save and another cannot in the same
folder, even with the same permissions on the old file.

\section{Change the smallest possible thing}
\label{sec:33-chmod}
\index{chmod@\texttt{chmod}}
\index{file mode}
Here is a reversible experiment. Symbolic \cmd|chmod| says who,
what operation, and which permission. \texttt{u} is owner,
\texttt{g} is group, \texttt{o} is other, and \texttt{a} is all
three. \texttt{+} adds, \texttt{-} removes, and \texttt{=}
sets exactly the listed basic permissions for that class.
An empty list following \texttt{=} clears the bits. A comma
separates clauses applied to the same mode.

\begin{terminal}
chmod u=rw,go= ./folder/budget.txt
ls -l ./folder/budget.txt
\end{terminal}

The basic mode now starts with \texttt{-rw-------}. The file
remains readable by its owner, but grants no group or other
access in its mode bits. The entire path is under your
practice directory, so this is not a live test involving another
account. \cmd|chmod| changes a mode, not ownership, and gives
no guarantee that previous copies are private. If private data
has already been shared, restrict further access as appropriate
and consult the responsible person; changing a mode cannot
recall an exported copy.

\begin{terminal}
chmod u-w ./folder/budget.txt
ls -l ./folder/budget.txt
cat ./folder/budget.txt
chmod u+w ./folder/budget.txt
\end{terminal}

Removing owner write doesn't remove read. \cmd|cat| should still
print the original text. The final line restores write.
When you need an exact mode for your own new file, you might
see numeric forms. Three ordinary octal digits encode the
owner, group, and other sets, in that order. Add 4 for read,
2 for write, and 1 for execute or search. Thus 6 is read plus
write; 7 is all three. Zero does not mean ``leave the group
alone''; it clears that group's basic bits.

\begin{quicktable}{M{.73in}YY}
\toprule
Mode & Meaning on an ordinary file & Typical directory consequence\\
\midrule
600 & Only owner read and write. & Owner can list names but lacks
search.\\
700 & Only owner read, write, and execute. & Owner can list, traverse,
and change entries.\\
644 & Owner read and write; others read. & The three classes lack
search.\\
755 & Owner all three; others read and execute. & Others can list
and traverse, not write entries.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Now remove just the owner search bit on your practice
subdirectory, not on the outer practice directory. The outer
directory stays searchable so you can name the subdirectory
to restore its mode. Do not try this on your home directory
or a system path.

\begin{terminal}
chmod u-x ./folder
ls -ld ./folder
cat ./folder/budget.txt
chmod u+x ./folder
cat ./folder/budget.txt
\end{terminal}

An ordinary unprivileged account should get a permission error
from the first \cmd|cat|, and the original contents from the
second. Note that \cmd|ls -ld| can show the directory itself
even when you can't go through it. If the first \cmd|cat| succeeds,
check what account and filesystem you are using rather than
tightening permissions on other directories. ACLs, protected
system areas, sandbox rules, file flags, and read-only mounts
can also affect actual access. If a change doesn't do what you
expect, locate the boundary first.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Can I make it read-only and relax?}
\Brian{If I remove write from the file, nobody can delete it, right?}
\Stephen{That protects the contents from an ordinary write.}
\Brian{But deleting it would surely write something somewhere.}
\Stephen{Yes: it changes the directory entry, not the file bytes.}
\Brian{So a directory I can write is enough?}
\Stephen{Not quite: it also needs search, and sticky rules may apply.}
\Brian{Then read-only is neither impossible to remove nor a backup.}
\Stephen{Exactly. Keep the version you need somewhere independent.}
\DialogueConclusion{File contents and directory entries differ.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Start new files with sensible defaults}
\label{sec:33-mask}
\index{umask@\texttt{umask}}
New files don't necessarily inherit the exact mode of their
directory. A process has a \FirstUse{file creation mask}, the
bits removed from the mode requested at creation. \cmd|umask|
prints or sets that mask. This is a filter, not subtraction;
clearing a bit that was never requested cannot add it.
Programs normally request up to \texttt{666} for ordinary
data files and \texttt{777} for directories. The new mode
depends on the program's request and the environment, not
just these typical starting values.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-33/mask.tex}
\caption{In this example the mask removes group and other access
from the modes requested for new objects.}
\label{fig:33-mask}
\VisualDescription{Requested 666 and 777 pass through mask 077,
producing 600 and 700 respectively.}
\end{figure}

\begin{terminal}
umask
( umask 077; mkdir ./private; printf '%s\n' demo > ./new.txt )
ls -ld ./private ./new.txt
umask
\end{terminal}

The parentheses run the commands in a subshell so they can
change the mask without changing the interactive shell's
original setting. On an ordinary local filesystem without
extra inherited access rules, new \texttt{private} should
be mode 700 and \texttt{new.txt} mode 600. The two plain
\cmd|umask| outputs should match. Run it only once: the mode
filter does not retroactively change an existing file, and
rerunning the redirection could truncate it. Restoring the
old mask does not restore modes of files created meanwhile.

Figure~\ref{fig:33-mask} uses the same filter for two
different requests. A mask of 077 doesn't make the text
file executable: its starting request lacked those bits.
Conversely, using a permissive mask doesn't override
a tool that always asks for owner-only access to its
settings. This is why the useful test is to create a
fictional object with the program you intend to use and
inspect the result, rather than treating the mask setting
as a full description of what is safe to share.

Linux ACLs, macOS ACLs and file flags, and BSD variants all
have additional access mechanisms. If a normal file
still refuses a write, check the exact error, parent path,
and filesystem before changing a mode. A recursive
\cmd|chmod| across an unfamiliar tree can remove required
access or make confidential files readable, with no record of
previous modes. Instead record the old mode, test one disposable
copy, and make only the specific intended change. Do not
attempt to work around system policy by adding administrative
privileges or making everything writable.

On macOS, permissions on the file and permission for the
application are not always the same question. Privacy controls
may restrict an app's access to locations such as Desktop,
Documents, or removable volumes. A command run in one terminal
app can therefore give a different result from a command run in
another, without any change in the mode shown for the file.
On macOS, the local \ManPage{ls}{1} documents \cmd|ls -le|
for showing extended security information such as ACL entries.
An ACL is a list of additional access rules on the object; the
nine basic bits don't spell out that list. Use a private test
directory outside protected folders to learn the basic rules,
and then report the exact wording and location category of a
real denial. Don't conclude from ``Operation not permitted''
alone that the mode is wrong, and don't grant Full Disk Access
just to make a practice example work.

The difference also matters when sharing a copy. Moving a file
to another folder on the same filesystem commonly preserves
its mode, whereas copying can create a new file with defaults
or preserve source permissions if the copy operation requests
that behavior. A private directory is useful, but after making
a file outside it, inspect the access on the new file and its
enclosing directory rather than assuming that it is the same
as the original. When using a network share or removable drive,
server permissions, mount settings, or the filesystem format
may also mean that visible mode bits don't act like those on
your local home directory.

\section{Investigate a failed save without trying it again}
\label{sec:33-denied}
If a file opens but won't save, copy the exact error into
a private note and preserve any unsaved changes through the
editor's Save As or recovery feature. Choose a new location
you already control; check that the new version is readable
before closing the editor. Then decide whether you meant to
change the original or only make a personal copy. You might
have read access to a shared original specifically so its
contents can't be changed by accident. The appropriate
outcome in that case is a personal working copy or a request
to its owner, not a more permissive mode on the shared file.

If the original is yours to edit, inspect it and its parent
separately. The practice pair below is safe to repeat after
the directory search bit has been restored:

\begin{terminal}
ls -l ./folder/budget.txt
ls -ld ./folder .
\end{terminal}

The first line asks how the existing file is protected; the
second looks at its directory and the current directory.
The two operands to the second line request both modes
without listing their contents. In a real investigation
there can be more than two components. Start with a
directory you know is accessible and inspect the next
one along the path. A later operand that the command
can't resolve does not invalidate the earlier listing.
If a path goes through a symbolic link, consider also the
directories on the target path. The link name doesn't
give permission to bypass access checks on its target.

Separate ``permission denied'' from ``read-only filesystem.''
If the filesystem itself will not accept writes, mode
changes cannot fix the destination. Likewise, an experiment
in a private local directory does not establish the behavior
of a cloud-synced or network-mounted folder. Testing with a
new fictional file on the affected storage is useful only
once you know you have permission to create things there,
and when creating it won't conflict with other people's
work. If the owner or policy is unclear, give the observed
error and your intended action to the person responsible.

For documents you mean to share with a household or
team, ask which group or service is supposed to supply
access. ``Other'' means accounts outside the owner and
applicable group class; on a shared computer this can
include people outside that team. Giving everyone read
permission to make one person's error go away changes
the audience, not just the symptom. On a system with
managed access it may make more sense to put a copy in
the team's approved location, verify what is shared
with a fictional file first, and keep the private version
under your control. Check the actual mode of the new
copy, since a privacy assumption from the source directory
can stop applying the moment you place it elsewhere.

On the other hand, owner-only mode is not encryption.
It is an access rule the running system applies to its
users. It doesn't make a storage device safe to lend to
someone or protect credentials passed in command arguments.
Do not use a permissions exercise to test security with
real confidential material. If you need to preserve
private data, consider the sensitivity and storage policy
of any backups too. Restore one practice copy first to see
which permissions your normal backup tool preserves.

\begin{exercise}{Show that read and write are separate}
\label{ex:33-read}
On \texttt{budget.txt}, remove owner write but keep owner read.
Check both with a long listing and a read. Restore owner write.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|chmod u-w ./folder/budget.txt| and
\cmd|ls -l ./folder/budget.txt|. After the earlier mode
practice, the file begins \texttt{-r--------};
\cmd|cat ./folder/budget.txt| should succeed. Restore owner write with
\cmd|chmod u+w ./folder/budget.txt|. This is not a reason to
test on a real budget.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Close a doorway temporarily}
\label{ex:33-door}
Remove owner search on \texttt{folder}; explain why you can
still list its mode but cannot open the inner file. Restore
search and verify the file is readable again.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|chmod u-x ./folder|, then \cmd|ls -ld ./folder|
and \cmd|cat ./folder/budget.txt|. The long listing looks at
the directory itself; opening the file needs search on that
directory. Use \cmd|chmod u+x ./folder| to restore it,
then repeat the read. No file content should change.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Use a mask without keeping it}
\label{ex:33-mask}
Use a mask of 077 only inside a subshell to make a new
file called \texttt{extra.txt}. Confirm the owner access
and check that your original mask did not change.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Read the original with \cmd|umask|. Run the following in the
practice directory and compare the final mask.
\begin{terminal}
( umask 077; printf '%s\n' practice > ./extra.txt )
ls -l ./extra.txt
umask
rm -i ./extra.txt
\end{terminal}
Expect 600 in the basic mode on an ordinary local filesystem.
The removal requests confirmation because \cmd|-i| was specified.
Confirm only the practice file that you just made.
\end{solution}

At the end, check that \cmd|pwd| still shows the practice room.
The following removes only the named practice files, with
confirmation, and the empty subdirectories. \cmd|rmdir| refuses
to remove a nonempty directory. \cmd|cd ..| returns to the parent
where the relative \cmd|"$room"| pathname was originally saved.
If you added files of your own, keep the practice room until
you decide what to do with them.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./folder/budget.txt ./new.txt
rmdir ./folder ./private
cd .. && rmdir "$room"
unset room
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Directory search and file read are both needed along an ordinary
path to readable data.
\item Inspect, record, change narrowly, and verify; a mode is not a backup.
\item \texttt{umask} filters requested access at creation, not afterward.
\end{takeaways}

